Date: Thu, 18 Nov 93 00:32:04 PST
From: pierre@shell.portal.com (Pierre Uszynski)
Subject: META: Charging $ for the List

> From: davisd@auburn.ee.washington.edu
> 
> >   If you want to ``freeload'' (no offense), find someone who has paid
> > for the list and get them to forward you messages via .forward.
> > 
> > -- Ray Cromwell        |    Engineering is the implementation of science;    
[...]
> it would probably make sense to allow for "freeload" senders who can
> post to the list but don't receive posts directly from the list,
> charging them an even more modest fee than the current minimum.

That's what I proposed weeks ago, at the time of the first charging
discussions and the "charging poll". There was not much response except
for the claim that

1) the load was more from running the database that the list is built
on than from mailing...

2) people would be concerned about privacy: reflected lists could not be
guaranteed to be kept secret, as they would be run by un-trusted Joe
Randoms...

3) people would be concerned about over-wide propagation of list
material (leaks).

I must say that I'm not satisfied by what little response I have gotten
from the list management, and that may be my primary reason for not
wanting to pay ANY fee:

Point 1 REALLY surprises me... And in particular, it seems to me most
subscribers do not use ANY list feature. They use the list only as a
relay, and would receive exactly the same functionality from a
reflector. Let users of fancy features pay for them if they want. For
me, local procmail processing is just fine (when I use any at all).

Point 2 and 3  are not compatible: today, anybody can subscribe to the
list, the only difference with a newgroup is the limitation of the number
of subscribers due to the difficulty of learning about the list. Whether
the list of subscribers is secret or not does not matter as I expect most
subscribers are not really known by anybody else on the list. People
concerned with the consequences of over-wide distribution of
controversial material would be better advised to use anonymous remailers,
rather than rely on any list confidentiality.

So, in conclusion, I believe a few levels of indirection can solve
this problem just like any other :-) That fees are being implemented
before a network of reflector lists is one reason to argue that the
real reason for them is not to compensate for computer load.

Pierre.
pierre@shell.portal.com

