Date: Thu, 18 Nov 1993 01:01:18 -0800
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: Drugs: questions/discussion

Thanks to everyone who offered their personal experiences and
knowledge, and sometimes help.  I have a lot of questions and
comments here.  I've put responses to many people in this message.
(Private messages will have to wait for now -- I've read and written on
this topic for about 5 hours now, wanting to handle the list messages
as a group, and need to get some sleep.)

Sameer wrote:
	There are those who use LSD just because it's cheaper
	than anything else. ("$3 and I can get fucked up for 14 HOURS!!! Wow.
	Sign me up!")

Can one really buy LSD that cheap?
I assume that if one can, that the product has little or no QC.
Can one get hi-Q LSD, and how much more would that cost?
Does the dosage of LSD affect the length of the trip or its intensity
or both?

	If you're "petrified" at the thought of lack of control, and
	associate self-transformatory drugs with loss of control, I'd suggest
	that you *not* do anything until you are fully convinced that you will
	stay in some control, with your chosen chemical, with a good trusted,
	experience group of people. (It's a good idea to have one person as
	"ground control", yes.)

I get this.  I also get that trusting myself is even more important
than trusting the other people (I recall a sort of encounter group I
participated in at one time, where, supposedly as an exercise in
safely exerting all my energy, four people attempted to hold me on the
ground, one per arm and one per leg.  They couldn't do it.  I found
this gratifying, but not very comforting.).

How commonly does a group of friends get together for such an
experience and have someone actually volunteer to be "ground control"?
I would expect everyone to want to participate.

Sameer writes, re MDMA:
	It's an empathogenic ego-softener, not a dissolver.

What does that mean?

Walter writes:
	I've done LOTS of drugs and I never drank Drano or broke furniture -
	that's really an exaggeration of the effects of most recreational 
        drugs.

I live an exaggerated (high peaks, deep canyons) emotional life
already.  That you haven't done these things doesn't convince me that
I wouldn't. 

Has anyone found that one drug or another actually _ameliorated_ their
destructive (including self-destructive) tendencies?

	By now, I've mellowed - alcohol or marijuana is enough to  
	loosen up a "social" situation :). 

I don't think I have a problem with inhibitions in that sense.  In any
case, I have no intention of ever getting drunk, and would probably
have to get more accustomed to marijuana use than I have any intention
of getting before I'd trust myself to take care of someone else as
well as myself while under its influence.

At a first guess, I'd think it might help just by reducing performance
anxiety.  Does this jibe with people's experience?

Andy writes:
	It is instructive to examine the effects of LSD and the like on the
	senses etc.  However, use of these under less than ideal circumstances
	can adversely affect the  normal survival-preserving behavior that
	humans have evolved. 

This is, at base, what I'm worried about.

	"Smart drugs" are another issue entirely,  but only raise
	possible toxicity concerns as opposed to the extreme care that
	needs to be taken for the safe use of psychedelics.

Actually, I worry about these, too.  I feel anxious about the idea of
any chemical altering my brain chemistry.  At the same time, I know
that the brain chemistry changes all the time, just from sleeping,
getting excited, or eating food.  Can anyone suggest a concise,
readable book on brain chemistry for the layman?  No thousand-page
treatises, please.

Elizabeth Schwartz writes: 
	I took LSD a few times and found it very educational, especially...
	and the accidental introduction to neuro-linguistic
	programming I got when I accidentally reprogrammed myself.

I don't like the sound of "accidentally reprogrammed myself".  Please clarify.

>From Sameer:
	my impression of MDMA is that the reported neurotoxicity is reversible.

How can that happen?  To me, a "toxic" substance means a substance
that kills, and as far as I know, death doesn't reverse.

	(I think there's [a study] going on in Southern California with those
	who have use large quantities of MDMA in nightclub/rave settings, to
	see what sort of serotonin levels they have.)

Here I need that book on brain chemistry.  Quickly (?), what does
serotonin do?

	The reports, to my knowledge, of nuerotoxicity, refer to
	reduced serotonin levels in the brain after the experience, for a few
	days, and a slight amount of axon deterioration, which I *think* grows
	back.

"Axon deterioration"?  I thought brain cells didn't regenerate.

	(MDMA as sold on the street & at raves is often just speed + LSD.)

Given the illegality of the substances involved, and the lack of
competing cryptoanarchic reputation markets, this seems an
ever-present danger.

	Someone having a bad trip is best in the company of a group of
	close, loving friends, not a hospital.

I'd make that close, loving, _knowledgeable_ friends.  If a
freaked-out friend showed up on my doorstep, I wouldn't know what to
do.  Casting about in the dark, I'd probably try food and water, a
stuffed animal, and classical music.  If the friend was violent, I
don't know what I'd do.  Click.  I just remembered -- for a year I
lived with someone with multiple personalities, who often exhibited
suicidal and paranoid behavior.  I talked this guy out of suicidal
states over the phone (ten hours at a stretch, long-distance) and in
person, and had to fight to keep him away from the kitchen knives more
than once. Once he tried to get out of a car as I drove it at 55 down
Highway 101 (I stopped him, but totalled the car in the process).  I
imagine that those incidents color my idea of what people mean when
they say that drugs can mimic psychosis.

	If one is not in the company of	a group of close, loving
	friends, it's unwise, in my opinion, to use any psychedelics, anyhow.

I have only one friend whom I consider quite that close and loving,
and inconveniently enough, he works in Alcohol and Drug Abuse
Prevention Counseling.  I also respect him highly (and want his
respect, perhaps too much), and I think that having a rational
explanation for him of why I wanted to try a drug experience would
constitute one sign of my readiness to do so.

	(As you might be able to tell from my refs to it here, PIHKAL
	is a *good* book.)

Does PIHKAL stand for anything?

Ray writes:
	I don't like
	losing control and really don't see the point of doing drugs which
	don't improve your performance.

As I understand it, properly used, psychedelics can improve your
performance, by allowing you to reprogram yourself for greater
effectiveness, which would last after the pharmacological effect of
the drug had worn off.  If I didn't think this might be the case, I
would probably not see any point to them either.

	I also don't fool around with mind/emotion altering drugs like LSD.
	Scientifically, we don't know what possible side effects that
	they could have on your firmware....
	I'd prefer to see LSD improved and tested by the market first 
