Date: Thu, 18 Nov 93 4:09:20 EST
From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu
Subject: META: Charging $ for the List

Pierre Uszynski writes:
> That's what I proposed weeks ago, at the time of the first charging
> discussions and the "charging poll". There was not much response except
> for the claim that
> 
> 1) the load was more from running the database that the list is built
> on than from mailing...

   The load is two fold: (1) delivering messages to users (2) computing
costs associated with list features. Both are variable costs plus
fixed costs associated with the panix account and the disk usage.

> So, in conclusion, I believe a few levels of indirection can solve
> this problem just like any other :-) That fees are being implemented
> before a network of reflector lists is one reason to argue that the
> real reason for them is not to compensate for computer load.

   We are not going to pay for a reflector. You find a machine which is
willing to pipe 17+mb of material per day, you set up and administrate
a reflector list, and you let us know who is on the list (otherwise
they won't be able to post), and I'll make sure you get a feed
(minus those whose messages contain a X-Reposting-Policy header and those
who quote such such messages) if Exi ok's it.

  Even if all of the delivery were offloaded from panix, cpu would still
cost us $4-6/yr per user. Every posted message consumes 10-20 seconds
of CPU time. People who didn't pay for the list might not be able to post
because they would be subsidized by those who are paying in the form of
increased costs. 

  You might say "why not just scrap the list software and go to a dumb
reflector with no cpu usage?" I say, ExI and Harry have invested their
money in the list software development for the improvement of Extropian
e-mail service. Some people don't like this, I have received mail from
many people who do. Let the market decide. I'll do what I can
and what ExI let's me, to give the scrooges ;-) free or extremely cheap
list access but I am not promising anything. My time is valuable --
I wouldn't be devoting so much time to the software except that I was paid
to. I have 3 exams next week ;-)

  However, I think it needs to be said that many people are going to be
in a state of withdrawal soon when the commercial sector of the internet
takes over. People are so used to getting things free, subsidized by
the govt and many sysadmins's phone/electric bills, that weaning them
off of free internet is going to be a tough experience.

  Maybe extropians is the first e-mail list to go commercial, but if we
didn't do it, somebody would have. (Clarinet has proven to me that
commercial internet information becomes far superior than the free access
groups)

