  Certainly my position if taken to its extreme could support that or any
other viewpoint, but I'm not advocating going that far. My position in
regards to e-communication is, in order to facilitate certain discussions, it 
is sometimes neccessary to restrict the domain of subjects to some small base
of ideas which are assumed true.  It's not that an argument should never be
analyzed, but that sometimes it should be dismissed for sake of efficiency.
Often because it is repetitive (e.g. "I have a new design for a perpetual
motion machine"), but frequently because it is not even relevent.
(e.g. David Sternlight posting to cypherpunks arguing against
crypto being in the hands of private citizens, or a priest posting
to a gay dating service preaching against homosexuality. Or Detweiler
on pseudospoofing. Cypherpunks would be much better if people simply
dismissed his arguments and refused to even debate them. Regardless of
whether or not subsequent Detweiler arguments were more lucid. He is simply
disruptive.)

  It's not as simple as saying "your argument is objectively incorrect, qed",
more like "I do not want to even see your argument unless you can briefly 
summarize why it is different than X, Y, and Z, all of which I have seen 
before and disagree with" or "your argument is not valid by the assumptions
of this forum. Please take this to alt.basics were some brave individuals
will argue this."

  Sometimes debating the basics might help refine them, but replacing the
extropian basics with a new set of basics would simply erase the existence
of extropianism and give the label to some other philosophy. 

