Date: Thu, 18 Nov 1993 13:46:07 +0000 (GMT)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: ("Straight talk about") Drugs

mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu (John McPherson) writes:

>Disclaimer: What I write below is in no way intended to be an advocacy
>  or encouragement of using any of these drugs.
        :

Interesting account excised.

It might be of interest to the list to know that I've experimented or
been subjected (involuntarily) to some of the substances John hasn't
taken (and vice versa). Here's _my_ two cents:

>Drugs I haven't experimented with:
>barbiturate - both Marilyn Monroe and Jimi Hendrix died of overdosing
>  on this one (combined with alcohol).  I have no desire to try it.

It's also worth noting that a bolus injection of phenylbarbitone is
used as a means of execution in some parts of the US. I've actually
been through something not entirely unlike such an execution -- in my
case, for eye surgery requiring _deep_ anaesthesia -- the difference
being that there was an anaesthetist on hand to keep my lungs working.
I got such a large dose that I remember very little, except a
cold feeling in my arm followed by a weird sense of dissolution from
the inside out. Then absolute nothingness ...

Barbiturates in general have a low therapeutic index (i.e. ratio of
ED50 to LD50 -- effective dose for 50% of population to lethal dose
for 50% of population). They've generally been superseded as medical
tranquillizers by the benzodiazepines, which are much less toxic but
_HIGHLY_ addictive.

>heroin - I've heard it described by an ex-junkie turned naval officer
>  as: "sexual orgasm times 10 stretched out over a period of 4 minutes".
>  It's also supposed to be fairly addicting and often cut with 
>  undesirable substances, and since I don't like needles anyway, I have
>  no particular desire to try it.

Diacetyl morphine, or heroin, is simply the most easily prepared
opioid derivative of morphine (extracted from opium). Doses are about
a quarter that of morphine, and the bioavailability varies. Despite
WoD propaganda diamorphine has a medical role in the control of
myocardial infarction (heart attacks), and is used in the treatment of
acute life-threatening heart attacks and terminal cancer in the UK and
other countries.

I was once taken ill in Thailand with respiratory problems. A doctor
was summoned: the quack mixed a cocktail of ampoulles together,
botched an injection into my right elbow, got it right in my left
elbow, then left a couple of unlabelled packets of pills. (Bastard
expected a tip, too, despite conduct amounting to gross medical 
malpractice. As it turned out later, I was suffering from heat
stroke!)

Anyway, it turned out that what he'd pumped into me was a mixture of
multivitamins and a fast-acting opiate. I spent the next twenty
minutes or so mainlining on something not a million miles from heroin.

The verdict?

Well, this stuff really _is_ scary. The description of it as better
than an orgasm is accurate. It was better than the best orgasm I'd
ever had, or had since. I also felt good about things. I was so
blissed out that even though I was conscious and aware I was unable to
rotate my wrist and eyeballs far enough to see my watch and track how
long the experience had lasted. 

Then I began to come down. I was alone (my partner had gone to pay off
the quack) and it was really rather unpleasant; reality began to
intrude in waves of grating ugliness. I had a powerful need to renew
the experience of warm joy and pleasure, while at the same time being
conscious enough to be scared shitless by the implications of the 
experience. (I resolved then and there that I would never, EVER,
accept the offer of a syringe full of the stuff; another shot and I
might find myself carrying a monkey.)

It's worth noting that after the eye surgery mentioned about they gave
me a shot of morphine. However, due to the pain (I guess) there was no
euphoria or pleasure associated with it; it was just a useful pain
killer. 

So the point is: heroin (and analogs) give an incredibly pleasurable,
intense trip. You remain consciously present (unless you take too
much) while experiencing direct stimulation of the pleasure pathways 
in the brain. In addition to the physiological addictive properties it
must have very strong habit-forming ones. (I would imagine that trying
to kick a major habit would be a bit like trying to forgo all
forms of sexual experience.)

Heroin should actually be very cheap to buy (if it was
legal) -- it's not an expensive drug to manufacture -- but I wouldn't
advise anyone to take it, or any other opioid. Certainly not more than
once ... unless you can live with the idea of being like a rat with
the pleasure centres of its brain wired up to a switch.

>tranquilizers (Valium, Librium) - since these are legal, surely someone
>  you know (maybe even on this list) can tell you about them.
>etc.

Benzodiazepines (including diazepam (vallium) and others) are pretty
effective means of blissing out. In low doses you get a warm,
comfortable feeling and any low-key anxiety you feel dissolves in a
cloud of "so what?" In higher doses you tend to fall into a deep,
dreamless sleep. I'd call them harmless fun (they tend not to be very
toxic, either) except that there's a disturbing body of evidence that
they're actually apallingly addictive. Heroin withdrawl peaks at 48
hours and lasts up to a week. Benzodiazepine withdrawl is more mild,
but lasts a cumulative six weeks/year of use; a ten year habit takes
over a year to shed! Moreover, the withdrawl symptoms are just about
the opposite of the drug effects; a heightening of anxiety, neurotic
fears and phobias, paranoia, twitchiness. I once met a man who'd tried
to withdraw from oxazepam 30 times over a ten year period, and who had
failed every time. The last time he had tried, he told me, he had
become agoraphobic: convinced that the street would try to eat him if
he went outside his home. 

As if that isn't bad enough, addiction can set in after as little as a
month or so of use -- according to the current received wisdom among
the pharmaceutical profession in the UK.

