Yes, Hayakawa, and Berman, and a few others identify themselves as
GS-icists and most people find them easy to read. No pain, no gain?
Korzybsky's writing seems opaque at first because he uses a large set
of new terms extensively before defining them. Also, the unabridged
_S&S_ contains an enormous amount of detail; you definitely want
_Selections_ instead.

  I just want enough to work with that I can feel
|> I'm playing Ellis's game in good faith.  He manages to get rid of
|> _all_ his "to be"s -- no mean task.  Try it sometime; if you don't
|> find writing that way enough of a challenge, try holding spoken
|> conversations with _no_ "to be"s in them -- no progressives, no
|> compound verbs, none.  I tripped over myself in almost every sentence
|> for about an hour and a half, then gave up.  I may find it easier once
|> I've had more writing practice. 

I have written a fair amount of E' and E-choice; I don't try to speak
it, although I have heard some who do speak fluent E' in real-time. I
consider that basically a stunt.

[...]wondered why "is going"
|> would warrant any different treatment than "has gone" (Equality for
|> All Auxiliary Verbs!).  I feel relieved and gratified that I can
|> achieve consistency and still follow the rules of E-prime (as
|> expounded by list members) by including both rather than by excluding
|> both.

If you leave in the "innocuous" forms of "to be", you have chosen
E-choice rather than E'. I suppose much depends on whether you
intend to benefit more from the end product or from the learning
process. I like E-choice better, but when I write it I often end up
with E' anyway. I once ended an article with "written in E-choice by
choice" and when I later reread it I found only one little "be"
buzzing around in 200 words, and that one a harmless auxiliary.

[...]
|> It occurred to me this morning that this "question" really has an
|> imperative meaning:  people use it to mean "Stop doing that!" when
|> knowing what you are doing has little or no value for them.  I prefer
|> that people say what they mean (this may have to do with my put-on
|> defenses being so weak).

Exactly. GS texts point out that _people_ have meanings, not words. A
sentence in your mind with its associations and intentions symbolizes
a complex meaning; when you speak or write it, you have translated it
down from symbol to signal; someone receives the signal and creates
their own symbol -- necessarily different from the sender's symbol.
A.K. called this process "semantic reaction" and a large part of GS
training focuses on making it a deliberate process so that we pick up
more of the speaker's "unspokens" before responding, and so we have a
feel for how complete the symbol mapping was.

|> BTW -- does Korzybski have as much of an argument with "to become" and
|> "to exist" as he has with "to be"?

It's important to note that Korzybsky did not invent or endorse
E-prime, it was a student of his (Borland, I think -- I had to shed
the GS books from my briefcase to make room for _Applied
Cryptography_). Koryzybsky depended on other devices to get away from
the "is of identity". E' has developed into a sideshow, and judging
from recent issues of _Etc._, one that will not readily go away.

