walter kehowski <wak@next11.math.pitt.edu> writes:
>####
>Charlie Stroos says:
        :
>Around the mid-80's the government began to get concerned about the
>``threat to the fabric of society'' that these travellers represented.
>This was code for the fact that, having no fixed abode, the travellers
>could not easily be hunted down and forced to pay the poll tax.  A media
>campaign accusing them of vandalism and damage to property was
>orchestrated pretty thoroughly, and at the same time a fairly heavy
>program of police harassment was instituted.  During the 80's the social
>security regulations in the UK were tightened considerably, and
>entitlement to benefits was withdrawn from several categories: including
>people who didn't live at a fixed address for long enough to register.
>####

>My reply:
>The rave culture in England also were not too respectful of private property  
>(littering, sewage, vandalism) and the citizenry complained. The proper function of  
>govt. is to protect the citizen against such encroachments. I would not discount a  
>tax evasion concern, however.

Well yes, but ...

Firstly, there were attempts to organize legal raves on private
property rented for that purpose. The police found excuses to ban
these (inadequate compliance with health and safety laws, failure by
the organizers to guarantee that drug dealers would be excluded, that 
kind of thing). The entire rave culture was seen as some kind of
threat to law and order. A friend of mine who was involved in the
"summer of love" (1989) mentioned one rave near Liverpool attracting
nearly 75,000 people; many of them using MDMA and other drugs. (Recent
surveys indicate that among under-25's in the UK, about 50% have tried
cannabis and >15% regularly use illegal substances.) These events were
fundamentally frightening to the establishment because (a) raves
indicated the existance of a huge youth sub-culture over which they
had no control, and (b) there was a politically/culturally subversive
element to them (free love, drugs, shamanistic trance states) that was
at odds with the approved cultural self-image to which the authorities
subscribe (clean cut, family values, hard-working, no altered states
allowed). 

More to the point, the poll tax rebellion was not limited to the
travellers; it was just a pretext for cracking down on them. (In
Scotland in the last year of the poll tax there was a shortfall of
>50% of owed local taxes. Entire districts refused to pay and
threatened to lynch bailiffs sent to confiscate the property of people
charged with tax evasion.) 

One of the characteristics of the current Conservative government in the
UK is that its rhetoric doesn't match its behaviour.  Saying "we
advocate freedom to choose" and "privatise all state monopolies" is all
very well, but the government sells the monopolies off as units
(creating de facto _private_ monopolies like British Telecom and the
local Water companies), and attacks all forms of local democratic
government that aren't controlled by their own party.  They also
consolidate central bureaucratic control wherever possible, with the
apparent overall goal of establishing a party hegemony.  (As witness
changes in the electoral regions designated by the Boundary Comission,
the elimination of the Metropolitan County Councils, the introduction of
political appointees to civil service jobs that were traditionally
non-partisan, and the use of the poll tax to drive poor voters off the
electoral register.)

Despite income tax being much lower now than ten years ago, the overall
tax burden in the UK is _higher_ (due to a sales tax levied on virtually
everything at 17.5%).  This weird mix of non-interventionist ideology
and centralist corporate statism is augmented by lip-service to
conservative family values of the most backward, knee-jerk variety;
``family values'', thinly-veiled little-englander racism and xenophobia,
anti-feminism, para-christian religiosity, attacks on any sub-culture
that shows signs of non-conformism, and a general sense that anything
that is not expressly permitted is forbidden.

For example, despite homosexual acts between consenting adult men over
21 in private having been legalized in 1968, prosecutions for homosexual
acts are actually running at a _higher_ level now than before
legalization due to police/judicial reinterpretation of the meaning of
the word "private".  For _another_ example, many pagan families have to
keep their religion a secret from school teachers and social services --
for fear of having their children taken into care on the specious
grounds that the parents might be abusing them.  And for another,
individuals who participate in S&M or B&D encounters in private can now
be prosecuted for "inflicting grievous bodily harm", regardless of
whether all participants consented (following the "Spanner" prosecution).

The result is a kind of grey, consensus morality that is being stamped
like a boot across the face of anyone who looks for something
different. If it isn't "moral" it isn't allowed. If it's different,
it's viewed with deep suspicion and may be banned. And although the
talk is all about empowering people with freedom to choose, the
actions are to constrain and limit individual freedom.

You may gather that I dislike the current British government and you'd
be right. There's a thin layer of quasi-libertarian propaganda grafted
on top of an archaic, backward-looking conservativism that is
intensely anti-progress, anti-diversity, and paternalistic. I've posted
in the past about how I am not a libertarian, and suspect libertarian
policies would not lead to a utopia overnight, but frankly I'd _much_
prefer to see a libertarian government in power in the UK than the
current bunch of self-serving hypocritical suits.


-- Charlie


